# 37. The registry and publishing

A package on your machine installs from its directory. This chapter is how
a package reaches other machines: the repository that holds the official
packages, the registry that names them, and publishing a package's crate
to crates.io.

## Official and known packages

[`hql-extensions`](https://github.com/ewiger/hql-extensions) is the
repository of official packages. It is a Cargo workspace with one crate
per package under `crates/`, and one file at its root,
`hql-packages.yaml`, the **registry**.

| Kind | Where its crate is kept | Who maintains it |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **official** | under `crates/` of `hql-extensions` | the HQL project |
| **known** | anywhere, pinned by the registry to a crates.io version or a git revision | its author |
| a path package | on your machine | you; it is in no registry |

The registry is a list of names. It decides what `ml` means when someone
writes `hql package install ml`, and it is reviewed, because a package is
native code with the rights of the `hql` process
([[35-how-a-library-is-loaded/static-and-dynamic#what-a-library-is-not|chapter 35]]).

## A registry entry

```yaml
registry: 1
packages:
  - name: ml
    crate: hql-ext-ml
    kind: official
    version: 0.1.0
    hql: ">=0.7.0, <0.8.0"
    provides: [ml, ml.metrics]
    description: Datasets from records, linear and logistic regression and k-means as values, prediction, and metrics
```

| Key | States |
| --- | --- |
| `name` | the package's name, unique in the registry and distinct from every builtin package |
| `crate` | its crate, `hql-ext-<name>` |
| `kind` | `official` or `known` |
| `version` | the version the entry pins; for an official package the tag `<crate>-v<version>` of the repository |
| `hql` | the `hql` versions the package builds against |
| `provides` | the modules it provides, all under its own name |

The `hql` key is read before anything is fetched. A package whose range
excludes the running `hql` is refused there, with both versions named.
The same range is in the crate's own `Cargo.toml`, as `hql = "0.7"`.

## Version ranges and exact versions

Two version statements concern a package, and they answer different
questions.

| Statement | Where | Answers |
| --- | --- | --- |
| a range, `hql = "0.7"` | the crate's `Cargo.toml`, and `hql:` in the registry | which `hql` versions is this source written for? |
| an exact version, `hql = "0.7.0"` | `[build]` in an installed `package.toml` | which `hql` was this library built against? |

A crate is published with the range. One release of `hql-ext-ml` serves
every `hql` 0.7, because each install builds it again against the `hql`
that runs the install. The exact version exists only in a build
([[35-how-a-library-is-loaded/the-version-rule|the version rule]]).

## Installing by name

`hql package install ml` reads the registry, checks the entry's `hql`
range, fetches the crate at the pinned version, and then does what
`install --path` does: builds it against the running `hql` and installs
the result.

```sh
hql package search metrics
```

```text
ml 0.1.0 (official, ml, ml.metrics)
  Datasets from records, linear and logistic regression and k-means as values, prediction, and metrics
```

```sh
hql package install ml
```

| Command | What it does |
| --- | --- |
| `hql package install <name>` | install the registry's pinned version of a package |
| `hql package install <name>@<version>` | install another released version of an official package |
| `hql package search <text>` | registry entries whose name, modules or description hold the text |
| `hql package update [<name>]` | install the version the registry now pins, where the installed one differs |
| `hql package registry [--refresh]` | where the registry is read from, when it was fetched, and how many packages it lists |

The source is kept under `~/.hql/src/<name>/`, which is what `rebuild`
builds again. For an official package it is a checkout of the repository at
the entry's tag, holding that one crate; fetching it needs `git`.

The registry is fetched once a day and kept under `~/.hql/registry/`. A
command that needs it uses the copy while it is less than a day old, and
also when the fetch fails, so a machine that is offline still lists and
rebuilds what it has. `hql package registry --refresh` fetches it now.

A package installed from a path is not the registry's: `update` leaves it
alone, and `rebuild` builds it from its directory.

## Publishing a crate

A package's crate is published to [crates.io](https://crates.io) under its
crate name, so that a pinned version of it can be fetched by anyone. The
`Cargo.toml` of a published package states what crates.io requires and
what ships:

```toml
[package]
name = "hql-ext-ml"
version = "0.1.0"
description = "HQL package `ml`: datasets, fitted models as values, prediction and metrics"
license = "MIT"
repository = "https://github.com/ewiger/hql-extensions"
readme = "README.md"
include = ["/src", "/modules", "/examples/*.hql", "/examples/hql.toml", "/README.md"]

[dependencies]
hql = "0.7"
```

The module root is part of the published crate: `include` lists
`/modules`, since a crate without its contracts is not a package.

Three rules follow from how Cargo publishes:

- **`hql` is a version range from crates.io.** A crate with a path or git
  dependency cannot be published, so the `hql` version a package names is
  on crates.io before the package is.
- **Everything else is ordinary.** A package depends on any crate, and
  those dependencies are built on the installing machine with the rest.
- **A crate does not enable features of the crates it shares with `hql`.**
  The values the two exchange hold types of those crates, and a feature
  can change a type
  ([[35-how-a-library-is-loaded/the-version-rule#every-refusal|every refusal]]).

`cargo publish --dry-run` builds the crate as crates.io would, from the
packaged files alone, and is worth running before a tag:

```sh
cargo publish -p hql-ext-ml --dry-run
```

## A release of an official package

In `hql-extensions` a package is released by a tag:

1. Set `version` in the crate's `Cargo.toml` and in its registry entry.
2. Merge to `main`. CI tests the crate and runs every program under its
   `examples/` against a build of `hql`.
3. Push the tag `<crate>-v<version>`, `hql-ext-ml-v0.1.0`.

The release workflow checks that the tag, the crate and the registry
entry state one version, and runs `cargo publish` for that crate alone.

## Registering a package of your own

A known package is added by a pull request to `hql-extensions` that adds
one entry to `hql-packages.yaml`. The review checks that:

- the crate is named `hql-ext-<name>`, the name is one lowercase word no
  other package uses, and the modules are under that name;
- `[package.metadata.hql]` states the package, its modules and its module
  root, and the contracts under the root declare those modules;
- the entry is pinned, to a crates.io version or a git revision, and
  states the `hql` versions it builds against;
- the crate builds and its examples run against those versions.

A package nobody registers is still a package. It installs with
`--path`, and a team shares it as it shares any repository.

## Prebuilt libraries

!!! warning "Design only"

    This section describes functionality that is not in the current release.
    It is written as if it existed, and names may change before it ships.

Building from source needs a Rust toolchain and a minute. A **prebuilt
library** removes both for official packages: each `hql` release is built
with one exact compiler, and the official packages are built with that
same compiler for that same release, per platform, and published with
checksums. `hql package install` then fetches the library that matches
the running `hql` exactly, verifies its checksum, and falls back to
building from source where none matches.

The version rule is unchanged. A prebuilt library is one that happens to
have been built for this `hql` already, and the header check of
[[35-how-a-library-is-loaded/the-entry-point#the-header|chapter 35]] holds
it to that.

